diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
+++ b/README.md
+todo archive                        # move done items last changed over 30 days ago to the archive, see Archive
+todo board [--overdue] [--snoozed] [--tag TAG]... [--priority P] [--project NAME] [--open|--done] [--doing] [--search TEXT] [--sort priority|due] [--archived]   # todo, doing and done side by side
+todo export --format csv|md|ics [--out FILE] [--overdue] [--snoozed] [--tag TAG]... [--priority P] [--project NAME] [--open|--done] [--doing] [--search TEXT] [--sort priority|due] [--archived]
+todo list [--overdue] [--snoozed] [--tag TAG]... [--priority P] [--project NAME] [--open|--done] [--doing] [--search TEXT] [--sort priority|due] [--archived]
+todo search TEXT [--overdue] [--snoozed] [--doing] [--project NAME] [--archived]   # same as todo list --search TEXT
+Shared filters: `--overdue` and `--snoozed` work on `todo board`, `todo list`, `todo search` and `todo export`, next to `--tag`, `--priority`, `--project`, `--open|--done`, `--doing`, `--search` and `--sort`. `--doing` shows only items marked doing (see Doing). `--overdue` shows only open items past their due date. A snoozed item is hidden by default from `todo board`, `todo list`, `todo search`, `todo export` and `todo remind`; `--snoozed` shows only the snoozed ones instead. `--project NAME` shows only items in that project, ignoring case (see Projects). `todo search TEXT` takes only `--overdue`, `--snoozed`, `--doing`, `--project` and `--archived` (the same as `todo list --search TEXT`), so `todo search TEXT --snoozed` finds a snoozed item.
+
+`--archived` is shared the same way: `todo board`, `todo list`, `todo search` and `todo export` read the archive instead of the todo file, and every other filter applies to what they find there (see Archive).
+`todo board` prints the items in three columns side by side: `todo`, `doing` and `done`. It takes the same filters as `todo list` (`--overdue`, `--snoozed`, `--tag`, `--priority`, `--project`, `--open|--done`, `--doing`, `--search`, `--sort`, `--archived`) and writes nothing. An item gets into the `doing` column with `todo start ID` (see Doing).
+- It reads only the todo file, so archived items leave the counts: after `todo archive` moves a done item out, its project shows one fewer done and one fewer total (see Archive).
+- Archived children drop out of the plain `done/total` count, since the count reads the todo file; `todo list --archived` counts the children found in the archive.
+### Archive
+
+`todo archive` moves every done item whose last change was more than 30 days ago into the archive, kept in `$TODO_FILE.archive` next to the todo file. It prints `archived N` (`archived 0` when nothing is old enough, and then it writes nothing). Open items never move, however old, and neither does a done item with no last-change stamp (one made before stamps were stored). The age is the local date of the item's last change against today, so an item done exactly 30 days ago stays and one done 31 days ago moves. Items move with every field and their last-change stamp unchanged.
+
+```
+$ todo archive
+archived 2
+$ todo list --archived
+[x] 1: file taxes
+[x] 3: call the bank
+```
+
+`todo list --archived` lists the archive instead of the todo file, with the usual `todo list` lines. The filters apply to it (`--tag`, `--priority`, `--project`, `--search`, `--sort`, `--done`, `--open`, `--overdue`, `--snoozed`, `--doing`). `todo search TEXT --archived`, `todo export --archived` and `todo board --archived` read the archive the same way. Without `--archived` nothing reads the archive for a listing: `todo list` no longer shows archived items, and a damaged archive file does not stop it.
+
+- Ids are never reused: a new item takes an id above every id in the archive too, so the number of an archived item is never given to another one.
+- History keeps its credit: streaks, XP, achievements, stats, review and burndown read the todo file and the archive, so archiving a done item changes none of their figures (see Streaks).
+- `todo show ID` finds an archived item. `todo note`, and every other command that changes items, works only on the todo file: an id found only in the archive exits 2 (`todo: error: no item 1`).
+- `todo projects` reads only the todo file, so archived items leave its counts.
+- An open subtask of an archived parent lists at top level, and an item waiting on an archived blocker stops waiting, since only an open item in the todo file blocks (see Subtasks and Dependencies). In `todo list --archived`, a parent and its archived children nest as usual, and the `done/total` count covers the children found in the archive.
+- A damaged archive file stops `todo archive` (even when nothing is old enough) and `todo list --archived` with exit 1, and nothing is written. `todo list --archived` does not read the todo file, so a damaged todo file does not stop it.
+- `todo archive` writes the archive first, then the todo file. If the second save fails, the item sits in both; the next `todo archive` replaces the archived copy and removes it from the todo file.
+- Nothing brings an item back from the archive.
+
+- Archiving does not sync: `todo sync` merges only the todo file, so an item you archived comes back from the remote, under its old id, on the next sync while any synced copy (the remote included) still holds it, and it then sits in both the todo file and the archive until `todo archive` runs again. Archiving every local copy does not stop it: a sync pushes the item to the remote file too, and any copy that still holds it sends it back. Only archiving the remote file as well (`TODO_FILE=$TODO_REMOTE todo archive`) keeps it away. The archive itself is not synced.
diff --git a/tests/cli/test_cli_archive.py b/tests/cli/test_cli_archive.py
